WEBVTT

00:00:00.007 --> 00:00:03.923
LLM API design should make request and response structure explicit.

00:00:03.923 --> 00:00:08.050
A chat endpoint usually accepts system, user, and assistant messages,

00:00:08.050 --> 00:00:09.975
model selection parameters, generation settings,

00:00:09.975 --> 00:00:12.000
and possibly tool or structured output options.

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.000
A completion endpoint may accept a single prompt.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.975
An embeddings endpoint usually accepts text and returns vector representations.

00:00:18.975 --> 00:00:23.230
Clear schemas help clients send predictable requests and handle responses safely.

00:00:23.230 --> 00:00:26.255
Generation settings should be treated as part of the API contract.

00:00:26.255 --> 00:00:30.035
Temperature, sampling controls, max output length, stop conditions,

00:00:30.035 --> 00:00:32.000
and context limits can affect behavior dramatically.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:35.000
Some applications should expose these settings to trusted operators,

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.000
while others should enforce server-side defaults.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.020
If clients can change everything,

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:42.255
troubleshooting becomes difficult and safety assumptions may no longer hold.

00:00:42.255 --> 00:00:46.180
Streaming responses are useful when users benefit from seeing partial output as it is

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:47.200
generated.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:51.055
Streaming can improve perceived responsiveness, but it also complicates cancellation,

00:00:51.055 --> 00:00:54.000
timeout behavior, logging, error handling, and client code.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:58.055
A robust API should define what happens when a request is canceled, when the model stalls,

00:00:58.055 --> 00:01:02.200
when the client disconnects, or when the service cannot finish within expected limits.

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:05.000
Structured output expectations need validation.

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:10.000
If an application asks the model to return JSON, a classification, or an action plan,

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:13.720
the downstream system should still verify that the output is well formed, complete,

00:01:13.720 --> 00:01:15.000
and appropriate for use.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.235
Models are not parsers or authorization engines.

00:01:17.235 --> 00:01:22.120
Version API behavior as models and prompts change so clients do not silently inherit

00:01:22.120 --> 00:01:24.000
incompatible output formats or changed semantics.
